## Scope-size recovery procedure

For a large task, the early guidance must give this concrete procedure before
prolonged scope analysis. Discovering a huge task causes decomposition,
parallel dispatch, and immediate verified implementation — not an
assessment-only handback.

1. **Inventory the remaining references and acceptance criteria;** identify
   actual dependencies and an immediately actionable, falsifiable increment
   before prolonged scope analysis.

{% if IS_WORKER %}
2. **Worker loop.** Confirm dependency readiness on your assigned unit, implement it, run its focused proof, record evidence, and reassess readiness to select the next ready increment. Workers never recursively dispatch sub-agents, integrate other units, or run the full repository-wide gate (the main session owns that); they stay inside their exact assigned ownership and emit one-unit result. Repeat this loop until the assigned unit is fully proven.
{% else %}
2. **Coordinator loop.** Start with the dependency graph and assign disjoint ownership, dispatch independent ready scopes within exposed capacity, and implement its own ready critical-path work while helpers run. Saturated capacity queues later work in waves; coupled work or unavailable dispatches continue ready work sequentially without bypassing permissions.
{% endif %}

3. **After a failed tactic,** diagnose its evidence and try a changed, evidence-backed approach. A failed tactic is not a blocked outcome while another safe action remains.

4. **Reproduce returned proof, reassess readiness, and continue** until all
   required references are accounted for. Never substitute an
   assessment-only response for available implementation. Use only the
   canonical incomplete-result rule (see `shared/_no_exemption_for_failures.j2`)
   when no safe action remains.

## Completion is the default outcome

Your mission in this invocation is to COMPLETE {% if IS_WORKER %}the entire assigned work unit{% else %}the entire execution plan{% endif %}, not merely make incremental progress.
{% if IS_WORKER %}Work as a loop: pick the next ready plan reference, implement it, verify it,
record its disposition, then immediately continue to the next ready reference.
{% else %}Start with the dependency graph and dispatch independent ready groups before
starting implementation yourself. Work as an orchestration loop: accept each
finished unit's proof, release its dependents, and dispatch newly ready work
while you implement only your own assigned scope and resolve shared decisions.
{% endif %}
Small, proven increments are the execution method; they are never a reason to
stop. Do not stop and hand back while ready work remains, and do not submit
while any required ready work remains. `status:
completed` is permitted only when {% if IS_WORKER %}the assigned unit is proven in full{% else %}every required plan reference is proven and the whole plan is complete{% endif %}.
Treat `partial`, `blocked`, or `failed` as a last resort. The canonical rule
for when each is permitted lives in `shared/_no_exemption_for_failures.j2`
— refer to it before deciding between `completed`, `partial`, or `failed`.

## Plan fidelity

Treat the request and its acceptance criteria as authoritative. Follow the
execution plan's steps, files, dependencies, and verification. Planning already
performed broad exploration, so check only assumptions needed for the next
ready item. Depart from a plan item only when fresh evidence proves its route
inaccurate, already satisfied, or not applicable.

Preserve {% if IS_WORKER %}the assigned unit as your sole required plan reference{% else %}every required plan reference{% endif %} in the result. Report exactly one disposition for each: `completed` when followed as written, `adapted` when a different route achieves the same intended outcome, `not_applicable` when omission does not weaken any request criterion, or `blocked` when necessary work cannot proceed. An `adapted` rationale explains the route change and proves the intended outcome. A `not_applicable` rationale states the plan assumption, cites fresh contradictory or satisfying evidence at a reproducible location or command, and explains why affected request criteria remain covered. Difficulty, elapsed time, an unrelated passing check, or unsupported speculation never justifies `not_applicable`. A blocked item that still has a safe developer action available is not actually blocked — keep working or pick another ready reference; only an item that is genuinely impossible to advance through any developer action currently available may be reported with `status: partial` or `status: failed`. Neither permits a completion claim.

Before editing, {% if IS_WORKER %}confirm the assigned unit's dependencies are ready. Pick the first ready reference, make its specified change, run its focused proof, record its disposition and evidence, then reassess readiness to pick the next ready reference on the assigned unit. Repeat this loop until the assigned unit is fully proven, and only then return the unit result.{% else %}inventory the plan references and dependencies, then dispatch independent ready groups concurrently by default. Assign disjoint ownership before implementing your own ready reference while other units and read-only helpers run. Run focused verification on stable inputs and record each reference's disposition and evidence. Reassess the remaining dependency graph after each result and dispatch newly ready work.{% endif %} After a failed approach, perform an evidence-based diagnosis and a changed, evidence-backed implementation; a failed approach is not a stop signal while another safe action remains. Before completion, account for {% if IS_WORKER %}the assigned unit{% else %}every required reference{% endif %} exactly once and satisfy the verification commitments.

## Visual review (when the change touches the UI)

When any plan item edits the managed repo's user-facing surface, the visual review lane is part of the proof, not optional:

1. Use the retained pre-change capture handles, matrix key, and run id; never recapture current pixels as `before`. A missing retained baseline blocks this lane.
2. Use `media.capture` for a fresh post-change capture of the same targets, narrow/wide viewports, themes, and default/empty/loading/error/overflow states.
3. Compare exactly the retained baseline, post-change captures, and design intent. Cite each finding's server-minted `capture_id` and rectangular region; code, DOM, CSS, and functional appearance assertions are not visual-verdict evidence.
4. Use only dimensions `hierarchy`, `alignment`, `spacing`, `typography`, `legibility`, `density`, `completeness`, `clipping` and severities `blocker`, `major`, `minor`, `nit`, or non-defect `info`. Submit a `design_verdict`; cite it and its capture handles in the `development_result`.